      {
        "id": "char_1",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "QMMD",
        "is_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "QMMD simulations show Pt(100) work function of -5.06 eV corresponding to U = 0.62 V (close to experimental PZC of 0.41 V) and Pt-O first peak at 2.15 Å that decreases as U decreases from +0.29 to -0.46 V, weakening water adsorption.",
        "evidence_snippet": "The work function of water/Pt(100) predicted from QM-MD is -5.06 eV, which corresponding to U = 0.62 V ... close to the experimental potential of zero charge (PZC) on Pt(100) of 0.41 V ... Figure 2a shows the distribution of water perpendicular to the surface along the z-axis (ZDF), in which the first peak of Pt-O is 2.15 Å ... As U decreases, the height of the first peak of Pt-O decreases."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_2",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "QMMD",
        "is_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "QMMD shows water reorientation on Pt(100): 3 H₂O∥ (parallel, θ<30° or >150°) at U = +0.29 V reducing to 1 H₂O∥ at U = -0.46 V (pH 12.8), making surface more hydrophobic.",
        "evidence_snippet": "At U = 0.29 V, we find 3H₂O∥ as shown in Figure 2d,f ... Figure 2g shows the snapshot of water/Pt(100) interface at U = -0.46 V (pH = 12.8). In this case, only one H₂O∥ is observed."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_3",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "Widom insertion",
        "is_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "Widom insertion method predicts 0.13 eV increase in apparent hydrogen binding energy (HBE*) from pH 0.2 to 12.8 with slope of 10 meV/pH on Pt(100), matching experimental 8-12 meV/pH.",
        "evidence_snippet": "We predicted a 0.13 eV increase in hydrogen binding from pH = 0.2 to pH = 12.8 with a slope of 10 meV/pH ... The slope predicted from QMMD is 10 meV/pH, very close to 8 to 12 meV/pH as measured experimentally."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_4",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "XPS",
        "is_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "Predicted O 1s chemical shift for chemisorbed H₂O⊖ on Pt(100) is -0.18 to -0.88 eV relative to bulk water (506.66-507.36 eV), with H₂O⊖ at 507.54 eV.",
        "evidence_snippet": "We have calculated the O 1s chemical shifts of various H₂O molecules near the surface and found that the bulk water has O 1s from 506.66 to 507.36 eV, whereas the O 1s for H₂O⊖ is 507.54 eV. Thus, we predicted -0.18 to -0.88 eV chemical shift of O 1s."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_5",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "2PT",
        "is_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "Two-phase Thermodynamic (2PT) analysis shows H₂O⊖ on Pt(100) has ~300 cm⁻¹ blue shift in H-O-H bending mode and 25% lower entropy (38.06 J/K/mol) than bulk water.",
        "evidence_snippet": "We employed Two-phase Thermodynamic (2PT) model to predict the density of state (DOS) of water from QMMD ... we predict ~300 wavenumbers blue shift of H-O-H bending mode. The entropy of H₂O⊖ from the 2PT calculation is 38.06 J/K, about 25% decrease than that of bulk water."
      },
